<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Dual-purpose config:
   1. Process keep-alive — the binding alone gives the app the highest
      process-priority tier so BYD firmware won't kill it (original purpose).
   2. Physical-key mapping — flagRequestFilterKeyEvents + canRequestFilterKeyEvents
      let onKeyEvent intercept hardware buttons (steering-wheel / dash keys) BEFORE
      the OEM handler, so the user can rebind them. This is a standard a11y
      capability — no root, no signature permission, no INJECT_EVENTS.
   3. Seat-position capture — typeViewLongClicked lets onAccessibilityEvent
      notice the user long-pressing BYD's seat-position buttons
      (com.byd.carsettings:id/location{1,2,3}) to save that geometry into
      OverDrive's own position store. flagReportViewIds (already set) exposes
      getViewIdResourceName() so the match is by resource-id, not localized
      button text. The match reads the event source only — no getWindows(), no
      extra window-retrieval flags.
   4. Autostart auto-enable — AutoStartEnabler drives BYD's
      com.byd.appstartmanagement dialog through the a11y APIs.
      canRetrieveWindowContent + flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows are what let
      getWindows() reach that dialog's window: it is mShowToOwnerOnly, so
      getRootInActiveWindow returns the launcher and uiautomator cannot see it
      at all. canPerformGestures backs the dispatchGesture coordinate-tap
      fallback, and typeWindowsChanged notices the dialog appear — the enabler
      polls as its primary mechanism, so that event is belt-and-suspenders.

  Event types and flags are the UNION of (2), (3) and (4). notificationTimeout
  stays at the key filter's 100 ms: a longer value coalesces event delivery,
  which is wrong for a service that also filters hardware keys, and neither the
  capture hook nor the enabler depends on it.

  NOTE: onServiceConnected re-asserts eventTypes with a hard assignment, so the
  live mask is whatever that code sets — typeWindowsChanged declared here is not
  in it. The enabler polls getWindows() instead of relying on that event, so this
  stays declared for intent while the flags (which onServiceConnected only ORs)
  are what actually carry through.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeViewLongClicked|typeWindowsChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagRequestFilterKeyEvents|flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows|flagReportViewIds"
    android:canRequestFilterKeyEvents="true"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:canPerformGestures="true"
    android:notificationTimeout="100"
    android:settingsActivity="com.overdrive.app.ui.MainActivity" />
